{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that the preference costs only 'assurance of truth-likeness' understates the actual cost: it renders the preference practically inert for decision-making. By explicitly denying that corroboration predicts future success or probability (as the mechanism states: 'refusing to assign any weight to the future reliability'), the target admits the preference offers no rational basis to expect better outcomes than random guessing. If acting on Theory A over Theory B provides no expectation of superior results, the 'preference' is merely a ritualistic adherence to past test severity without pragmatic justification, failing to answer why a scientist should prefer it when stakes involve real-world consequences.","premise":"A rational preference for action must provide some expectation of improved outcomes compared to alternatives; otherwise, it is not a guide for action but an arbitrary rule.","premises_essential":["911046891c4aab0a557cd1f92632a6d2e7515f515aed78ce7495d051b6431e69"],"successor_question":"If corroboration implies no probability of future success, what non-inductive principle justifies betting one's life on a well-corroborated theory rather than a poorly tested one?","target":"911046891c4aab0a557cd1f92632a6d2e7515f515aed78ce7495d051b6431e69"}]}